<!--
  The RC's internal joystick - "DJI Virtual Joystick", 2ca3:1501 - is the one device the
  app can never do without. Declaring it is what puts the "use for this device by
  default" tick into Android's USB permission dialog. Without a declaration the dialog
  has no tick, and the grant it hands out does not survive a reboot, so the app had to
  ask again after every one (see RcRawJoystick, which is the reason the app wants the
  device at all).

  The two RF modules (see RcModuleSerial) are declared for the same reason:

  303a:1001 is the internal module: the ESP32-C3's native "USB Serial/JTAG", a standard
  CDC-ACM device (class probe, see CdcAcmSerialDriver) that is wired inside the RC.

  1a86:7523 / 1a86:7522 are the external module: a USB-UART adapter (WCH CH340),
  where the module in the bay-style serial port is connected. 7523 is the classic
  PID and is the one measured working here; some CH340 variants enumerate as 7522.

  The other common USB-UART bridges are declared too, so swapping the adapter for a
  CP210x, FTDI, PL2303 or a classic CH340 does not ask for permission again:
  10c4:ea60 (SiLabs CP210x), 0403:6001 (FTDI FT232), 1a86:7523 (WCH CH340),
  067b:2303 (Prolific PL2303).
-->
<resources>
    <usb-device vendor-id="11427" product-id="5377" />
    <usb-device vendor-id="12346" product-id="4097" />
    <usb-device vendor-id="6790" product-id="29986" />
    <usb-device vendor-id="4292" product-id="60000" />
    <usb-device vendor-id="1027" product-id="24577" />
    <usb-device vendor-id="6790" product-id="29987" />
    <usb-device vendor-id="1659" product-id="8963" />
</resources>
